WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:04.004
No matter what stage of transition you're in, 
no matter where you are in your life, 

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:08.591
you are worthy to be here. 
You are worthy of love, and you are lovable.

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:14.139
And we must claim the sacred spaces 
that are already here for us, 

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:17.100
that are waiting for us just to claim them. 

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.978
So claim your crown.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.531
Hey there everyone. My name is Tre'Vell Anderson 

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.492
and we are here to discuss Disclosure. 

00:00:34.492 --> 00:00:38.288
Joining us we have executive producer 
of the documentary

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:40.415
and one of the featured voices, Laverne Cox. 

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:42.250
We also have with us Angelica Ross, 

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.712
one of the other featured voices in Disclosure,

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.339
and you all know her from her work in POSE, 

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:51.009
American Horror Story 
and so many other things. 

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.635
Before we get into the film though,

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:55.472
I'd love to hear from each of you 

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.557
how you two became friends?

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.226
How you came into each other's orbit?

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.896
I'm wondering... yes, girl, 
how did we meet, girl? 

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.273
I believe it was 2013

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:08.068
and it was the first Trans 100.

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.737
I went to Chicago to support.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.864
And that's when we met for the first time.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:18.036
If I'm correct, you actually hosted 
The Trans 100 that year.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:21.915
I was the host that year. 

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:26.753
I was the host onstage 
and so I had my little audio equipment,

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:30.840
and I was doing interviews of people 
that were coming through beforehand, as well.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.386
And I remember when Laverne, 
I first met her, she had on her flats,

00:01:35.386 --> 00:01:39.974
Miss Ma’am had on a little sweater, 
very, very humble, 

00:01:39.974 --> 00:01:41.851
very humble pie coming in the door.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:42.894
She's like, "How can I help?"

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:45.688
I had finished shooting the first season 
of Orange is the New Black.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.733
It had not come out yet, 
because the Trans 100 was in March.

00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:53.279
Orange didn't come out till July. 
So yeah, I had shot it and it was coming out.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:55.073
I didn't know what was going to happen.

00:01:55.073 --> 00:01:57.242
And you were just so like, 

00:01:57.242 --> 00:02:01.162
"I'm here for community and what can I do to help?"

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:06.209
In such a way that, so much happened in a year

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:11.464
and the next year when you came back, it was like,

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:13.800
Laverne Cox! The Laverne Cox!

00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:17.595
Well, we're here to discuss Disclosure.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.722
Laverne, as an executive producer, 

00:02:19.722 --> 00:02:22.725
could you give us a little bit of background information

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:24.769
in terms of how you got involved with the project? 

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:28.773
Angelica is the reason I became involved in Disclosure.

00:02:28.773 --> 00:02:31.442
I was living here in LA and I was missing community

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:34.571
and I went online and I saw that you and Jen Richards

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:38.658
were speaking at a panel and I went because of you

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.703
and Sam Feder happened to be giving a presentation

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:44.122
of the research that would become Disclosure.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:48.585
I'd always wanted to do something that in my mind

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.254
I called it the Transgender Celluloid Closet.

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:55.383
For those who don't know, is a film 
that's based on the book by Vito Russo 

00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:00.722
that looked at the history of Gay and Lesbian 
representation in Hollywood.

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:04.100
For years I was like, 
"We need something like that for Trans people." 

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:07.228
What moments from your memory, 

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:09.230
your recollection of Trans history,

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.650
were very important for you to have included in the film?

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.655
So my first memories of seeing 
what I thought was a Trans character on screen,

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:21.951
which was Edie Stokes from The Jeffersons.

00:03:21.951 --> 00:03:26.080
It was also the relationship between drag, 

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.835
the history of sort of comedians,
particularly African-American comedians, dressing in drag.

00:03:30.835 --> 00:03:36.674
I knew we needed to unpack 
the impact of those representations

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:40.303
on the lives of Trans people and my life,
life as a Trans person specifically. 

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:44.766
Angelica, you smiled when she said The Jeffersons, 

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:46.517
Edie Stokes and The Jeffersons. 

00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:51.356
What's your connection to Trans imagery 
prior to this particular moment? 

00:03:51.356 --> 00:03:54.901
One thing we couldn't also find, 
I don't think, in the archives

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:59.030
was the fact that I had appeared 
on the Maury Povich Show twice.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.783
When we watch those shows Maury Povich, 

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:02.992
"Is it a he or a she?" 

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:04.160
When we watch those shows.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.870
The ones who were the most sickening

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.914
caught people's attention.

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.833
And so for us in the Trans community,

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:13.920
it was like the casting call of all casting calls. 

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.924
Maybe somebody might eventually cast you 
on something or what have you.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:19.634
That was our...to hope for.

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:23.429
And because we were taught by allowing the media

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:26.057
to do an exploitation into our lives, 

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:28.518
that was one of the ways we could be discovered.

00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:31.813
And now I'm so grateful that we have other avenues. 

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:36.276
As exploitative as they were, there's something so beautiful

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:40.238
looking back and seeing the show girls from the 90s

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:44.242
who were just epic and legendary and everything on these shows,

00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:48.329
even as they didn't necessarily teach the best lessons

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:51.207
to cisgender people about who Trans people were,

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:56.421
just the fact that we were there and we have evidence. 

00:04:56.421 --> 00:04:59.257
So now we have stories like POSE

00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:02.969
and other stories that are LGBTQ centered

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.555
that are finally including opportunities for us to show.

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:08.182
But what is so beautiful about what you just said Angelica

00:05:08.182 --> 00:05:11.978
and about this history is that we've always found a way.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:16.190
Whether there were opportunities or not, 
we created them,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.776
we made them happen. We made them work for us. 

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:22.155
One of the things that Disclosure does so beautifully

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:25.116
is it takes all of these very different images 

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:28.953
and very different moments and allows us as a community

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:32.623
to grapple with both kinds of 
the positives and the negatives, right.

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:38.046
It's been so beautiful though over the years 
seeing a Transparent happen

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:44.594
and really seeing a POSE happen and seeing all of the talent.

00:05:44.594 --> 00:05:47.555
Because for so many years, even before I broke through,

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:49.265
people were saying, there's no Trans actors,

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:50.558
and you can't say that anymore.

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.894
Laverne, could you give us a little bit of information

00:05:52.894 --> 00:05:57.815
about how you all put together the team 
that gave us Disclosure

00:05:57.815 --> 00:05:59.734
and the importance of that intention?

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:02.570
We were committed to making sure that everyone,

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:06.824
not only who's on screen in Disclosure is Trans, 

00:06:06.824 --> 00:06:10.161
but everyone who works behind the scenes is also Trans.

00:06:10.161 --> 00:06:12.914
And when we could not find someone Trans to fill a role,

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:16.376
Sam Feder, our brilliant director had an idea of creating

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:22.173
a fellowship program where that cisgender person 
would train a Trans person in a job.

00:06:22.173 --> 00:06:27.678
Everyone was invested in the material 
and in the subject matter in a really, really special way.

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:30.681
All of our interns got paid and that was really challenging.

00:06:30.681 --> 00:06:34.435
And so our budget was very different. 
Our budget model was really different.  

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:41.859
It is crucial that we have people, diverse folks, 
writers rooms and crews, behind the scenes,

00:06:41.859 --> 00:06:43.611
but how do we cultivate that talent?

00:06:43.611 --> 00:06:47.573
What are we going to do 
to provide opportunities for that talent

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:51.285
to cultivate themselves so that they're prepared 
when that call comes.

00:06:51.285 --> 00:06:54.539
Both of you are producing other work.

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:57.583
I'm wondering if what happened with Disclosure

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:00.086
and the way that people are responding to Disclosure,

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:03.840
has changed how you approach the work that you're doing?

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.301
For me, Disclosure was just further proof

00:07:07.301 --> 00:07:12.682
that Trans people being in charge of Trans narratives 

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:14.225
is a good idea.

00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:17.603
And so I want to be a part of creating a platform

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:21.566
and be involved in creating a space 
for my community to shine.

00:07:21.566 --> 00:07:25.903
What I feel is possible now, post Disclosure,

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:31.534
is for us to really have more 
sophisticated narratives around Trans people.

00:07:31.534 --> 00:07:34.120
One of the things that I love so much about the film

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.583
is the ways in which it kind of pairs transphobia with racism.

00:07:38.583 --> 00:07:43.129
I just wanted to start with you Angelica, about any reflections

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:48.342
that you have about what this film 
coming out at this particular moment says.

00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:53.264
What we're seeing with Disclosure 
is we can actually get through to people,

00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:59.937
if the lion is finally telling the stories 
instead of the hunter,

00:07:59.937 --> 00:08:02.064
that maybe we're not shooting and hunting lions.

00:08:02.064 --> 00:08:08.863
Now's the time where folks are starting 
to amplify what we've been saying all along. 

00:08:08.863 --> 00:08:16.120
There's so much to learn from Disclosure, 
not just about how we think about Trans people

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:19.248
but also how we think about women, 
and how we think about Black people.

00:08:19.248 --> 00:08:24.212
And I also believe that Disclosure gives us an opportunity 
to think about media in general

00:08:24.212 --> 00:08:29.842
and how it can teach us how to think about ourselves, 
about each other.

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.802
And that's not just about Trans people.

00:08:31.802 --> 00:08:36.390
That's about a lot of media that's coming at us right now, 
teaching us very specific things.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.685
And we have to, as citizens, as human beings,

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:45.691
have to be able to have a critical relationship 
to everything that we're receiving in the media.

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:51.489
And I think Disclosure gives us a wonderful template 
and frame with which to do that. 

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:56.285
Meanwhile, there's so much that's being done 
to try to erase us.

00:08:56.285 --> 00:09:01.749
It is up to us to never forget all of the Trans lives that we lost,

00:09:01.749 --> 00:09:04.627
never forget that they're counting on us 
to continue to make it better. 

00:09:04.627 --> 00:09:08.130
What the folks at the Anti-Violence Project have taught us

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:12.677
over the years is that the ways in which 
we end or mitigate, at least, 

00:09:12.677 --> 00:09:16.347
violence against Trans people, 
is that we need housing.

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:18.057
We need access to housing. 

00:09:18.057 --> 00:09:22.395
When you're homeless and Trans, 
you're going to experience violence way more.

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:25.189
That people need access to jobs.

00:09:25.189 --> 00:09:27.233
We need access to medical care.

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:32.029
These crucial material things, can help mitigate

00:09:32.029 --> 00:09:34.198
and maybe even end violence against Trans people.

00:09:34.198 --> 00:09:36.492
Obviously people need to stop killing us too.

00:09:36.492 --> 00:09:39.203
People need to get their stuff together to stop killing us.

00:09:39.203 --> 00:09:45.251
Statistically, we know that cis men, 
often cis men of color, who are killing us.

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:48.713
And I've said this and people have criticized me for it,

00:09:48.713 --> 00:09:51.882
but your attraction to me is not a reason to kill me.

00:09:51.882 --> 00:09:58.222
And so we need to be doing 
so much work with men in this country,

00:09:58.222 --> 00:10:02.560
and masculinity, so that they understand 
that their attraction to Trans women is not a reason to kill us. 

00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:08.357
I would love if both of you just gave a message to camera

00:10:08.357 --> 00:10:11.527
for our siblings out there in the community, 

00:10:11.527 --> 00:10:14.655
something that you would like them to remember. 

00:10:14.655 --> 00:10:18.951
There've been so many moments in my life where I said to myself,

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:25.374
"I'll be worthy after this surgery, or I'll be worthy after this 
landmark in my transition, I'll be worthy after this."

00:10:25.374 --> 00:10:29.503
But worthiness has no prerequisites. 
Worthiness is a birthright.

00:10:29.503 --> 00:10:31.922
You are worthy right now,

00:10:31.922 --> 00:10:34.300
no matter what stage of transition you're in,

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.510
no matter where you are in your life, you are worthy.

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:41.015
When I think about that there is no place 
where Blackness does not exist.

00:10:41.015 --> 00:10:43.017
We are everywhere.

00:10:43.017 --> 00:10:45.853
And so I think the very same thing about Trans people.

00:10:45.853 --> 00:10:50.274
It's equally as true across the spectrum of LGBTQ+ folks

00:10:50.274 --> 00:10:54.403
Not only should we as Trans people 
know that we exist everywhere,

00:10:54.403 --> 00:10:59.241
but so should society, because they think 
that we are relegated to the margins

00:10:59.241 --> 00:11:02.161
when I could be on the boat at that party. 

00:11:02.161 --> 00:11:06.207
I could be the doctor giving you your diagnosis.

00:11:06.207 --> 00:11:09.877
So we just have to know that Trans people exist 

00:11:09.877 --> 00:11:14.340
at all levels of society and we deserve to exist. 

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:17.259
Thank you both for joining us for this conversation. 

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:19.387
We truly appreciate it.

00:11:19.387 --> 00:11:21.639
Thank you so much. 
Thank you. 

